A||N||2||Jeff-87||Delco (UP3) Radio Blue plug will not fit?????||||5||
Z||000000||Jeff-87||07-18-2001||09:23 AM||||Hi, I am installing a Pontiac UP3 Radio and I bought the adpater from GM (#16140051).  When I tried to hook it up, the blue connector clip that holds the plug in will not fit.  The metal on the back of the radio is too close.  Has anyone else had this problem?  I tried searching the archives and did not find anything on this?  Also, of the plugs that connect to the car, are all 3 (12pin and 2-3pin) adapters used.  If this has already been covered, could you post a link to the topic.  Thanks for the help.<BR>Jeff||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jim Jimenez||07-19-2001||12:26 AM||||Sure, I use these all the time. The first couple I trimmed the plug to fit. The last one I just cut the tab off. With your late model up3, you will have to trim the side brackets ahd stretch a mounting hole. I also used about a eigth inch of spacing behind the brackets to bring the radio forward, since they are not adjustable. <BR>  Enjoy your new radio--They look sooooo good in a Fiero <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000002||tgowens||07-19-2001||01:59 AM||||you don't need to and shouldn't cut the latch tabs off of the plugs. use a small flat blade screwdriver and place the tip between the radio and the connector receptacle and twist to move the receptacle down while pushing the blue plug in place. It is a tight fit but that is for a reason.<P>Also, you should break out the triangular bracket in the back of the radio mounting area. This is due to the fact that the older 12 pin plugs on the stock radios were mounted vertically while the newer 21 pin plugs are mounted horizontally. when installing these radios, if you don't break that mounting bracket out it will push on one side of the power plugs and eventually pop it out of the receptacle and your radio will lose power.<P>As for the plugs, the long plug and the blue plug are the only ones used. The other plug is for the Chrysler radio and is not used in a Fiero application. The orange and black (or brown) plug is the radio power plug and mates to the stock receptacle from the wiring harness. All other plugs are unused.<P>And, if hooking this to the stock sub-woofer system you need to splice the two pink wires from the sub amp to the yellow wire from the car wiring harness in order to power the sub amp..........<P>Jim is also correct in the 1/8" spacing between the radio mounting brackets and the console frame........I use the little three wire spark plug spreaders behind the brackets, to space it out.......they are just the right thickness.....<P>Hope this helps........||||reg||1
